      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "76cd585b-1317-57d4-a2a6-ced91c28597a",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Dog breeds: English Water Spaniel, Estonian Hound, American Water Spaniel\n- People: Dr. Fred J. Pfeifer, F. J. Pfeifer\n- Organizations: Soviet Union national economy ministry, United Kennel Club, American Kennel Club\n- Places: New London, Wisconsin\n\nFacts:\n- The English Water Spaniel is an extinct dog breed, and the last specimen was seen in the 1930s after the breed disappeared in the early 20th century.\n- In 1947, the Soviet Union's national economy ministry directed that every country in the Union must have its own dog breed, and the Estonian Hound was bred in that context.\n- Dr. Fred J. Pfeifer saved the American Water Spaniel, established its breed club and standard, and helped it gain recognition from the United Kennel Club and later the American Kennel Club.\n- F. J. Pfeifer of New London, Wisconsin developed the American Water Spaniel as a registered breed.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "88c4c2e1-a3b2-59ef-b097-93aeb504d158",
        "text": "Page: English Water Spaniel. Sentence: The English Water Spaniel is a breed of dog that has been extinct since the first part of the 20th century, with the last specimen seen in the 1930s.\nPage: Estonian Hound. Sentence: It was bred in 1947 when the Soviet Union's national economy ministry decided that every country in the Union must have its own dog breed.\nPage: American Water Spaniel. Sentence: The breed was saved by Dr. Fred J. Pfeifer, who set up the breed club and standard, and whose work led to recognition for the breed by the United Kennel Club, and later, the American Kennel Club.\nPage: New London, Wisconsin. Sentence: The American Water Spaniel was developed as a registered breed by F. J. Pfeifer of New London.",
